NAME
    theme-check - hold an EmuSen theme package to its manifest's schema,
    the QML rules and the kit's tokens, then load its screens and audit
    their fit and reach

SYNOPSIS
    theme-check [--static] [--state DIR] <folder>...
    theme-audit [--gpu] [--state DIR] [--states LIST] [--catalogue FILE]
                [--section KEY] <folder>
    theme-audit --list-states

DESCRIPTION
    The development tool of the emusen.dev module for EmuSen theme
    packages (EmuSen_Qt.md §3.4, §3.7): a folder holding theme.json,
    screens/Home.qml, Consoles.qml, Games.qml and optionally Splash.qml,
    parts/ and assets/. It runs in two steps. The static step loads and
    draws nothing. The loaded step, run only on a package whose static
    step found nothing, is theme-audit, a Qt program of its own
    (Frontend/theme-check/audit), which theme-check runs as a process and
    whose lines it reads back: this module is linked into the
    standalone shell and its tree holds no Qt (decided 2026-11-08).

    Findings print one a line:

        file:line kind words

    the manifest's in line order, then each .qml file's in path order,
    and the last line is 'ok' or 'N findings'. The kinds:

        schema      theme.json is missing, is not JSON, lacks a required
                    key (schema, api, name, author, licence, variants,
                    screens) or has one of the wrong shape; a text scale
                    in fontSizes outside the kit's 1 to 1.3
        api         a schema number above 1, or an api newer than this
                    build's EmuSen.Theme 1.0 (a newer major, or a newer
                    minor of the same major); the words name both
        path        a file the manifest names (a cue, a preview) that
                    leaves the package or is missing; a quoted folder
                    import in QML that leaves the package or is not one
                    of its folders
        screen      a screen listed whose file is missing, or a screen
                    file present but not listed (it would never load)
        token       an override naming no token of the kit, or a value
                    not of the token's type
        contrast    a contrast pair of the kit under its floor once the
                    overrides are applied, in the variant that sets them
        note        a key schema 1 does not know: ignored, and not
                    counted (EmuSen_Qt.md §5.2)

    and the kinds of qml-rules(1) for each .qml file (function,
    callLater, literal, words, onCompleted, rows, import), under its
    theme profile: §1.3's rules as a view's, with the imports limited to
    the sandboxed engine's allow-list, matched whole: QtQuick,
    QtQuick.Layouts, QtQuick.Effects, QtQuick.Shapes, QtQuick.Controls
    and EmuSen.Theme, and quoted relative folders of the package. So
    EmuSen.Models, EmuSen.Kit, QtQml, QtQuick.Templates, QtMultimedia
    and Qt.labs are each a finding.

    Where each rule's facts come from. The schema is the crate
    emusen-theme-manifest (Frontend/theme-check/manifest), its one owner,
    which the theme host reads too. The QML rules and THEME_IMPORTS are
    qml-rules'. A token is a read-only property of the kit's Tokens
    singleton in the committed registry,
    Testing/qmltypes/EmuSen.Kit-1.0.qmltypes, with the type it declares
    there: QColor a colour written #RRGGBB or #AARRGGBB, double a number,
    int a whole number, bool true or false. The contrast pairs, their
    floors (4.5:1 for words, 3:1 for graphics; WCAG 2.1 1.4.3 and
    1.4.11) and the ratio are emusen-kit-core's (contrast_pairs,
    Colour::contrast). A pair is measured only when the theme overrides
    one of its two tokens, since the kit's own pairs are held by the
    kit's tests. A translucent override is composited before it is
    measured, as a compositor paints it: the surface over black, any
    other back over that surface, the fore over its back.

THE LOADED STEP
    theme-audit places each screen the package lists through the theme
    host (emusen-theme-host, EmuSen_Qt.md §3.5.1), the program's own
    route: a window holding the host and one slot, the package's folder
    given to the host, and the screen placed when the host is ready. A
    fresh engine is made for each screen. The views of EmuSen.Theme are
    then fed as the program's host feeds them, after the screen is
    placed, since the program places a theme's screens before its
    library is read; a theme must take the focus when its rows come.

    The library is synthetic and read through the real clients
    (emusen-client-fixture's world, the platform library built from the
    same commit, or the one EMUSEN_PLATFORM_LIBRARY names): eight games
    with made-up titles, two on each of four consoles, each played three
    hours apart, two hearted; a game paused on the NES for 12 minutes;
    one controller, 21:42, a battery at 64%. Choose a console is placed a
    second time over the same library grown to 120 consoles, as a player
    with many cores installed has it (decided 2026-11-08): the real ones
    and synthetic ones copied from a real row under made-up names, 40
    under one maker in all, ten under no maker, the rest under the
    table's other makers.

    The kit's Tokens of the theme's engine (each engine has its own
    singletons) are set to the big screen form with reduced motion on,
    so a resting motion stops and every state is at rest when read.
    Each screen is first held to stillness at 1280 x 800: once its
    animations stop, no frame may be swapped for 2 s. Then, in the dark
    and the light variant, at 1280 x 800, 1920 x 1200, 1920 x 1080,
    1280 x 720 and 3840 x 2160 (the gate's sizes, EmuSen_Interface.md
    §24.7), the harness of emusen-qt-test reads the window's item tree
    and walks it: from each operable element each of the four
    directions is pressed as Qt's arrow keys, which the kit's Frame
    answers as the pad's (EmuSen_Qt.md §1.7) and KeyNavigation answers
    natively, and what moved is put back. kit-core's rules then judge
    the state: fit (fit), pad reach from the focus along the walk's
    edges (pad_reach), pointer reach of what the walk reaches
    (pointer_reach), no desktop chrome (no_chrome) and contrast
    (contrast), with the measures of the big screen form. Contrast is
    measured, not read from the tokens: the harness hides every text,
    grabs the window once and samples each text's box on a grid, so
    words over a picture, a translucent fill or a gradient are judged
    against what is drawn behind them; kit-core composites the text's
    ink over each sample and needs WCAG 2.1's 4.5:1, or 3:1 for large
    type, over all but a tenth of the samples; a text with no words draws
    nothing and is not judged (the rule the program's
    own gate holds, EmuSen_Kit.md §7.10; theme-audit added it on
    2026-11-08, when its passes judged no theme's text). The static
    step's contrast holds the declared tokens alone. The crowd's states are judged in the dark
    variant alone, the pointer held to every element on the screen, and
    the pad's reach by a sweep in place of the walk: Up and Left until
    the focus stops, then each row Right to its end and Left to its
    other end, with Down between, as a player looks for one console
    among many. Both ends, since Down may land anywhere along the next
    row: over a design that goes Down to the nearest console in x, a
    sweep that walked each row one way heard 52 consoles of 120
    (measured 2026-11-08). A console counts as
    reached when the name a reader hears for the focused element
    (Accessible.name, else the first words under it) is its name and the
    element's centre is inside the window, so a
    theme that lays consoles out by hand, or names its tiles for no
    reader, fails it.

    The loaded step's kinds, each line naming the screen's file, line 1
    (Qt keeps no line for an item), the size and the variant:

        fit         a kit-core fit fault: past its panel or its clip,
                    overlap, cut at an edge with no fade, an ellipsis,
                    runs past its box, too small to read, and the rest
                    of EmuSen_Kit.md §3's fit rules; the items are named
                    by number, type, objectName or words, and box
                    An ellipsis is a fault only on the program's fixed
                    words (qt-models' words::is_fixed: the words of
                    words.rs and the shelves' names); a title, a name or
                    a phrase counted or built from them is data and may
                    elide, as a design's limits say (decided 2026-11-08).
                    The words are told apart by what a reader hears, so
                    a theme cannot mark its own words as data.
        reach       an operable element the pad does not reach from the
                    focus; for the crowd, the consoles the sweep did not
                    hear
        pointer     a click at an element's centre hits another, or does
                    not call what A calls; a list the wheel does not move;
                    never on the splash, which is watched and not operated
                    (decided 2026-11-08: its focus is the screen itself,
                    and what stands at its centre is the design's)
        chrome      desktop chrome on the big screen
        name        an operable element with no name a reader hears
                    (Accessible.name, else the words under it;
                    EmuSen_InterfaceSpec.md §4.3, kit-core's named), as a
                    Continue entry drawn with no game, or a tile whose
                    title is drawn only beside its cover
        contrast    words under 4.5:1 (3:1 large) against what the
                    grab shows behind them, in that state's variant
                    and size; the line gives the ratio and the text.
                    Two kinds of words are not judged. A place the
                    host declines (available false), found by the
                    name a reader hears, is an inactive component,
                    whose words WCAG 2.2's 1.4.3 exempts ("Text or
                    images of text that are part of an inactive user
                    interface component ... have no contrast
                    requirement"; https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/,
                    read 2026-11-09), but only while it is drawn
                    apart from its offered look: the place is read
                    declined, then offered (Library.show_declined,
                    the focus on it both times), on a grid of points
                    a design unit apart or less, and some point must
                    change by 3:1, the ratio 2.4.13 asks between the
                    same pixels of two states; else the line reads
                    "a declined place drawn as if offered" and its
                    words stay judged. 2.4.13's area (a perimeter 2
                    CSS pixels thick) is not asked: words dimmed to
                    38% in a 480 x 56 row change about 2% of it, the
                    ring 7.9%. No other dim words are exempt. Under a
                    held clock (the splash states), contrast is
                    judged at rest: a text whose ink, its colour with
                    its opacity, changed over the frame before the
                    held time (16 ms) is mid-change and not judged;
                    a text resting faint is still a finding.
        motion      a screen not still under reduced motion
        load        the host could not place the screen: the engine's
                    and the sandbox's words, a line each
        warning     anything Qt logged while the screen was shown, the
                    theme engine's warnings among them
        stopped     a pass the audit could not finish: the harness
                    panicked (a view still moving after 5 s), so the
                    state it names, by size and variant, and every
                    later state of that pass were not judged; the line
                    gives the harness's words and fails the check like
                    any finding (decided 2026-11-08: a pass that stops
                    is never silent, and the passes agree only when
                    both finish)
        recording   with --state, a picture whose size is not its
                    state's: the harness turns Qt's high-DPI scaling off
                    so one device pixel is a window pixel whatever the
                    screen's scale (a window on a desktop at 125% was
                    grabbed at 1600 x 1000 for 1280 x 800, and an
                    offscreen screen of 120 dpi over 96 the same,
                    measured 2026-11-08), and a picture that still
                    differs is a finding, not a quiet mismatch

    The Games screen is shown a section as the program's showBrowse
    shows one (emusen-theme-qt's browse::facts_of, the one owner of a
    section's heading, world, count and neighbours): the first console's,
    or the console or shelf --section KEY names, with its games, the
    focus on the first.

    --state DIR writes each state's recording into DIR as
    Screen-variant-WxH.spec and .png (EmuSen_InterfaceSpec.md's format),
    the recordings a theme's side-by-sides are made from; with several
    folders, each package's go into DIR/<its folder's name>. Each picture
    is taken once the screen rests after the walk's last put-back: no
    animation running but those that loop without end (a design's
    resting motion), or with none, no frame swapped for 100 ms; a screen
    that has not rested in 5 s is a stopped finding. The put-back moves
    the focus home, and a world that rises on a change of focus was
    caught half-faded (measured 2026-11-08, Horizon's Choose a console);
    a fixed wait would go stale under a theme's own longer transitions.

    --gpu (theme-audit alone) draws on the GPU: Qt Quick's Vulkan
    renderer in Wayland windows of a compositor the run starts for
    itself (kwin_wayland --virtual at 3840 x 2160, as the program's GPU
    tests run, emusen-qt-test's Compositor), stopped when the run ends.
    Its scale is 1 by construction, so the desktop's own scale and
    settings never reach a picture, and no window shows on the desk;
    the recording finding still guards each picture's size. Without it
    the run is offscreen on the software renderer, which draws no
    shader effect (a layer's mask or blur). The offscreen platform was
    not used for the GPU: it drives the RX 6800 through OpenGL but has
    no Vulkan instance, and its OpenGL window draws only its first size
    when grown (a 1920 x 1200 picture came out drawn in its lower left
    1280 x 800 alone, the rest black or stale; measured 2026-11-09).

    --catalogue FILE (theme-audit alone) places Choose a console once,
    over the synthetic library with FILE's consoles in place of its own,
    and judges it in both variants at the five sizes, the pad's reach by
    the crowd's sweep over those consoles; no other screen is placed. The
    states are labelled by the file's stem (Consoles-dark-n30-1280x800).
    A catalogue is plain text, one console a line, tab-separated: the
    maker (- for none), the name, how many games it holds, the engine it
    runs on, that engine's kind (emusen or libretro), and the table's
    console whose look (its body, hue and year) it takes; the last three
    may be left out, a console without its own look taking the first
    shelved console's under a made-up hue and year. # begins a comment.
    It is how a design's capacity states are recorded beside its canvas's
    #state= (decided 2026-11-08: one console, 30, 120, one maker of 40):
    Testing/themes/catalogues holds the canvases' own, eleven, one-console,
    n30, n120, maker40, long and empty, written from consoles.html's
    synthetic catalogue. A line it cannot read is named and nothing runs.

    theme-check finds theme-audit as EMUSEN_THEME_AUDIT names it, else on
    PATH; without it the command does not run unless --static is given,
    which stops after the static step. A package with static findings is
    not loaded, and a note says so.

    The audit has no glue of its own. The kit's Tokens of a theme's
    engine is found through emusen-theme-qt's host::kit_tokens, the one
    owner of that lookup (QQmlEngine::singletonInstance, which cxx-qt-lib
    does not bind); a second bridge of the same name would not link
    beside it (decided 2026-11-08). The screen's object is reached by its
    number in the harness's tree, which is its QObject's address.

    Proof, measured on 2026-11-08: the fixture packages under
    Frontend/theme-check/tests/themes, loaded is ok; cut-games places
    its count in window pixels at y 730 and is past its panel at
    1280 x 720; unreachable has a tile no walk leads to; by-hand lays
    its rows out as tall as they are, and with 120 consoles runs off
    every size. A package takes about 80 s, most of it the crowd.

STATES
    --states LIST records the states LIST names, comma-separated, or all
    of them for all; without it the run is the default alone. A state
    is fed through the theme's own surfaces as the program feeds them
    (Library.show with the places the host declines, Browse, Session,
    Device, the kit's Tokens), never by editing the theme's files, and
    each screen it names that the package lists is placed afresh and
    judged as the default is: the rules above at the five sizes in both
    variants, the recording and its size. Lines and recordings carry
    the state's name (Home-dark-nogame-1280x800). The list's one owner
    is the audit's states.rs; theme-audit --list-states prints it, a
    line a state (name, screens, words, tab-separated), and a test holds
    this section to it. One run for every state, since each run builds
    the synthetic library, draws its covers and starts Qt once.

    The default library's games have covers (decided 2026-11-09, so the
    default is the canvases' and nocover a state of its own): each a
    flat colour with its console and title, drawn by Qt Quick into an
    ES-DE media folder that the host is given as the theme's cover
    store, as the program's start gives it (emusen-qt-test's covers).

    default     the synthetic library with Fable of Tiles suspended;
                the splash finished under reduced motion; Choose a
                console again over 120 consoles
    nogame      Home with no game to continue
    empty       Home, Consoles and Games over Testing/themes/catalogues/
                empty.tsv: no console and no game; Games shows the
                All shelf
    one-console the synthetic library's NES games alone, one suspended
    one-game    Games over one-game.tsv: a console holding one game
    many        Games over many.tsv: a console holding 9,000 games
    nocover     the default library with no cover
    norecent    the same games never played and none hearted, no game
                suspended
    long        a ninth game whose title is too long for any place,
                played last and suspended
    pressed     Return held on the first focus, which a Walkable
                answers as A, while the state is judged and recorded
    disabled    Home with the Scraper declined by the host (its row's
                available false) and the focus walked to it by the
                pad along the walk's own presses; its words are held
                to contrast only if it is drawn as if offered
                (contrast, above)
    splash-1s   the splash with motion allowed and every animation's
                clock held 1 s after it is placed
    splash-3s   the same at 3 s
    splash-5s   the same at 5 s

    The capacity states (n30, n120, maker40) are --catalogue's. A held
    clock is the harness's (EmuSen_Kit.md §7.36): Qt's animation driver
    replaced by one moved only by the audit, a frame's 16 ms at a time
    from the placing, so a theme's splash is held as it is written and
    the theme API gains nothing. Under it the screen is placed afresh in
    each variant, since a change of variant animates colours that a held
    clock would never finish, and is recorded without waiting for rest.
    A Timer is not an animation and is not held. Letting the clock go
    leaves Qt's default driver in place of a threaded render loop's own,
    so the held states run last. The reference theme has no Splash.qml
    and no splash states: its splash is Moonrise, the kit's.

EXIT STATUS
    0 for no finding, 1 for one or more, 2 when it could not run (no
    folder given, a folder missing, an option unknown, theme-audit not
    found, or theme-audit unable to run).

ENVIRONMENT
    EMUSEN_THEME_AUDIT  the theme-audit program, where it is not on PATH
    EMUSEN_PLATFORM_LIBRARY, EMUSEN_TEST_CORE
                        a prebuilt platform library and test core for
                        the synthetic library; else cargo builds them

EXAMPLES
    theme-check Frontend/themes/emusen
    theme-check --static Frontend/themes/emusen
    theme-check --state /tmp/horizon Frontend/themes/horizon
    theme-audit --gpu --state /tmp/horizon-gpu Frontend/themes/horizon
    theme-audit --state /tmp/states --catalogue Testing/themes/catalogues/n30.tsv Frontend/themes/emusen
    theme-audit --states all --state /tmp/states Frontend/themes/meridian
    theme-audit --states nogame,splash-3s Frontend/themes/meridian
    theme-audit Frontend/theme-check/tests/themes/dim-picture

        .../dim-picture/screens/Home.qml:1 contrast 1280x800 dark: low
            contrast: <n> at <r>:1, needs 4.5:1 (<n> QQuickText caption ...)
        ...

    theme-check Frontend/theme-check/tests/themes/low-contrast

        .../low-contrast/theme.json:11 contrast accent on surface is
            2.13:1, under the 3:1 floor (dark)
        ...
        4 findings

